%S A401446 145,2041,6409,30769,63529,50881,194881,157441,497641,697849,1115209,
%T A401446 1210081,1693969,924001,3306769,2911609,4263649,6428161,3616561,
%U A401446 7545721,8643769,14633329,12697801,18306601,18940129,15198769,20486089,28977001,30154681,17871361
%N A401446 Square roots of the central entries in a six-square 3 X 3 magic-square construction from Pythagorean primes using a Fibonacci recurrence step on the legs.
%C A401446 For p = A002144(n), let q = A401445(n) and Q = a(n) = p*q.
%C A401446 There are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2. Let u = x^2 - y^2, v = 2*x*y, w = v/2 = x*y and C = Q^2. Here u is the odd leg and v is the even leg of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p, and q = (u^2 + (u+v)^2)/2 = (u+w)^2 + w^2.
%C A401446 The Fibonacci recurrence, started with v and u, gives the next pair u, u+v; see A401445.
%C A401446 Let X = u^3 + 2*u^2*w + 4*u*w^2 + 4*w^3, Y = -2*u*w^2, A = p*u*(u+2*w), B = 2*p*w*(u+w), d = 2*A*B, e = 2*X*Y and R = u^3 + 2*u*w^2 + 4*w^3.
%C A401446 Then the array with rows
%C A401446   [C+e,     C+d, C-d-e],
%C A401446   [C-d-2*e, C,   C+d+2*e],
%C A401446   [C+d+e,   C-d, C-e]
%C A401446 is a 3 X 3 magic square with magic sum 3*C. The six entries C+e, C+d, C-d-e, C, C-d and C-e are respectively (X+Y)^2, (A+B)^2, R^2, Q^2, (A-B)^2 and (X-Y)^2.
%C A401446 Here A, B and X are positive and Y is negative, so d > 0 and e < 0 for every p, and the same array is used throughout. It is the array used for e < 0 in A401004.
%C A401446 All nine entries are positive and distinct; see the linked note for the proof. The construction works whether q is prime or composite.
%C A401446 The terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of Q; for example, a(5) = 63529 > a(6) = 50881.
%C A401446 Since gcd(p,q) = 1, Q is a product of two distinct primes exactly when the corresponding p belongs to A401444.
%C A401446 For background on 3 X 3 magic squares with six square entries, see Morgenstern's discussion of Euler's formulas and Boyer's work linked below.
%H A401446 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/b401446.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A401446 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A401446 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A401446 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A401446 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A401446 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401446 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401446 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A401446 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are its odd and even legs, then a(n) = p*(u^2 + (u+v)^2)/2 = p*((u+v/2)^2 + (v/2)^2).
%F A401446 a(n) = A002144(n)*A401445(n).
%e A401446 For p = 5, the odd leg is 3 and the even leg is 4, so q = (3^2 + 7^2)/2 = 29 and a(1) = 5*29 = 145. The construction gives
%e A401446   [ 119^2, 205^2,  83^2 ]
%e A401446   [ 13753, 145^2, 28297 ]
%e A401446   [ 35161,   5^2, 167^2 ]
%e A401446 Every row, column and diagonal sums to 3*145^2 = 63075. All nine entries are positive and distinct, and exactly six of them are squares.
%e A401446 For p = 17, q = (15^2 + 23^2)/2 = 377 = 13*29 is composite and a(3) = 17*377 = 6409; the same construction again gives a magic square with six square entries.
%o A401446 (Python)
%o A401446 from math import isqrt
%o A401446 from sympy import primerange
%o A401446 def a401446(limit=400):
%o A401446     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A401446     out = []
%o A401446     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A401446         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A401446             continue
%o A401446         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A401446             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A401446             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A401446                 u, w = x*x - y*y, x*y
%o A401446                 # u is the odd leg; w is half the even leg.
%o A401446                 q = (u+w)**2 + w*w
%o A401446                 out.append(p*q)
%o A401446                 break
%o A401446     return out
%Y A401446 Cf. A000045, A002144, A002365, A002366, A070079, A070151, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445.
